# generic_sibling_method.tkr — gap #3 of the generic-stack completion (#163 follow-up): a stamped
# generic-class instance method (`Ctr__g__i64::twice`) calls a SIBLING instance method on `self`
# (`self.count() + self.count()`). Inside the abstract template that sibling call was typed against the
# abstract owner `Ctr`, so its `call_ns` named `Ctr`; the stamped `count` is emitted under the instance
# namespace `Ctr__g__i64`. Before the mono `call_ns` re-key, the two disagreed and the program failed to
# link natively ("undeclared identifier"). The re-key threads the instance-method Subst's owner→instance
# remap into the TCall's `call_ns` (and callee qualifier), so the call keys off the instance too. Seeded
# `n = 3`, so `twice` returns 6 — a non-zero value proving the sibling dispatch actually ran. Inert on
# the seed: the compiler corpus has ZERO generic instances, so the re-key is a no-op there.

Feature: generic_sibling_method — a stamped generic method calls a sibling instance method on self

  Scenario: sibling_self_method — `Ctr<i64>::make()` bound then `.twice()` re-keys `self.count()` to the instance (3 + 3 → 6)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario sibling_self_method: ok"

  Scenario: sibling_self_chained_factory — the chained `Ctr<i64>::make().twice()` spelling re-keys identically (3 + 3 → 6)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario sibling_self_chained_factory: ok"
